WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.447
A customer on a train taps send to transfer 45 euros.

2
00:00:06.447 --> 00:00:12.527
It goes to the API server, then through a broker to a consumer that writes the ledger.

3
00:00:12.667 --> 00:00:16.025
The transfer goes through, and the ledger gets one row.

4
00:00:16.025 --> 00:00:18.163
Then the reply is lost in a tunnel.

5
00:00:18.433 --> 00:00:21.866
The phone can't tell a lost request from a lost reply.

6
00:00:21.866 --> 00:00:25.999
After 10 seconds it does the only safe thing, and sends it again.

7
00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:31.525
The retry goes out on a new connection, and the server handles it as a new transfer.

8
00:00:31.525 --> 00:00:33.744
Now 90 euros have left the account.

9
00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:36.947
Each layer below has its own duplicate check.

10
00:00:36.947 --> 00:00:38.889
Look at what each one can see.

11
00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:44.733
TCP drops a copy of bytes it has already delivered, but only on one connection.

12
00:00:44.733 --> 00:00:46.729
The retry came on a new one.

13
00:00:46.933 --> 00:00:51.666
The broker drops a batch the producer resends with the same sequence number.

14
00:00:51.666 --> 00:00:54.904
The retry was a new request, so it got a new number.

15
00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:57.759
Each check is right inside its own layer.

16
00:00:57.759 --> 00:01:02.687
The duplicate only exists in what the customer meant: one tap, one transfer.

17
00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:04.645
Only the phone knows that.

18
00:01:04.645 --> 00:01:10.081
So it makes an id when the user taps send, and puts the same id on every attempt.

19
00:01:10.233 --> 00:01:17.237
Every hop carries it: an HTTP header, then a Kafka record header, then a column in the ledger row.

20
00:01:17.433 --> 00:01:23.627
The ledger makes that column unique, and inserts the row in the same transaction that moves the money.

21
00:01:23.833 --> 00:01:25.107
Run the retry again.

22
00:01:25.107 --> 00:01:27.845
It reaches the ledger carrying the same id.

23
00:01:27.845 --> 00:01:32.685
The insert finds it already there and does nothing, so the money moves once.

24
00:01:32.833 --> 00:01:37.787
That's the end-to-end argument: a check like this is only complete at the ends.

25
00:01:37.787 --> 00:01:40.484
The layers below make the common case fast.
